            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The Kingdom of Heaven, salvation, or ultimate spiritual truth which is worth sacrificing all earthly possessions to obtain.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Matthew 13:45-46\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Holy things, divine wisdom, or the Gospel that should not be offered to those who will mock or profane it.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 7:6\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Worldly wealth, vanity, ostentatious luxury, and earthly seduction, often associated with the Whore of Babylon or improper attire.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 17:4\",\"Revelation 18:12\",\"1 Timothy 2:9\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Heavenly glory, divine purity, and the entrance to eternal life, represented by the gates of the New Jerusalem.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 21:21\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The pearl is a strongly dualistic symbol in biblical literature. Through Christ's parables, it is universally recognized as the ultimate spiritual treasure. However, in the Epistles and Revelation, it frequently symbolizes corrupt earthly luxury and vanity, contrasted later by its use as the gates of the New Jerusalem, restoring its association with divine glory.\"}",
